==English==
{{wikipedia|Aldersgate#John Wesley|Aldersgate}}

===Etymology===
{{sense|religious experience}} Short for {{w|Aldersgate Street}}, the site of a Moravian meeting at which {{w|John Wesley}} had a religious experience.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# A [[ward]] of the [[City of London]]
# {{lb|en|historical}} A religious experience undergone by {{w|John Wesley}} in May 1738.
#* {{quote-book|title=Sojourner in the Promised Land: Forty Years Among the Mormons|books.google.com/books?isbn=0252025903|author=Jan Shipps|year=2000|passage='''Aldersgate''' is the expression Methodists use to refer to the powerful spiritual experience in which, in Wesley's own words, his "heart was strangely warmed."}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Methodist History|volume=43-44|page=268|books.google.com/books?id=8gfrAAAAMAAJ|year=2004|passage=Consequently, '''Aldersgate''' is perceived as one more spiritual experience along the way of salvation}}
#* {{quote-book|title=John Wesley and the American Frontier|page=150|books.google.com/books?isbn=1604771666|author=John Fremont Beeson|year=2007|passage=J. Earnest Rattenbury, in his book, Wesley's Legacy in the World, quotes a man by the name of Riggs, author of The Churchmanship of John Wesley, as saying that: the Wesley before '''Aldersgate''' was not the same man as after '''Aldersgate'''.}}
# A similar experience among his followers.
#* {{quote-journal|journal=Christian Advocate|edition=Pacific Edition|volume=87|page=565|books.google.com/books?id=_vgtAQAAIAAJ|author=Edward Laird Mills|year=1938|passage=Must our '''Aldersgate''' experience help us meet the needs of common men ?}}
#* {{quote-journal|journal=Michigan Christian Advocate|volume=70|page=xxii|books.google.com/books?id=srvmAAAAMAAJ|year=1943|passage=Now we know that only the road ahead can be our '''Aldersgate'''. And it can happen there, albeit, that road may lead through new battlefields of moral struggle for a Christian society before it emerges upon the heights of spiritual ecstasy.}}
#* {{quote-journal|journal=Motive|volume=23|page=16|books.google.com/books?id=LJBGAQAAIAAJ|year=1962|passage=We will not have had our '''Aldersgate''' until we can, on the one hand, confess with incredible joy that Christ has taken away our sins and then, on the other hand, plunge into the work of his kingdom}}

